<article class="env-remark" id="05PW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05PW">Remark <span data-tag="05PW">4.22.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. There exists a big category $\text{Ind-}\mathcal{C}$ of <em>ind-objects of</em> $\mathcal{C}$. Namely, if $F : \mathcal{I} \to \mathcal{C}$ and $G : \mathcal{J} \to \mathcal{C}$ are filtered diagrams in $\mathcal{C}$, then we can define </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _{\text{Ind-}\mathcal{C}}(F, G) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ i \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ j \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(F(i), G(j)).  \]
</div>
  <p> There is a canonical functor $\mathcal{C} \to \text{Ind-}\mathcal{C}$ which maps $X$ to the <em>constant system</em> on $X$. This is a fully faithful embedding. In this language one sees that a diagram $F$ is essentially constant if and only if $F$ is isomorphic to a constant system. If we ever need this material, then we will formulate this into a lemma and prove it here. </p>
</article>